   "title": "THE MATH OF INDEPENDENCE",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/20/the-math-of-independence/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 02:08:37 +0000",
   "_dt": 1792462117.0,
   "ageDays": 0.01,
   "summary": "That is what makes the idea of mental colonialism worth examining as Nigeria marks 66 years of independence. Colonial influence did not survive through one channel, several factors; some created during colonial rule and others expanded after independence account for this phenomenon. At independence, we inherited that extractive architecture where a state designed to control territory and collect revenue, not to serve citizens.",
   "summary_zh": "这就是为什么在尼日利亚迎来独立66周年之际，值得审视精神殖民主义这一理念的原因。殖民影响并非通过单一渠道延续下来，而是多种因素共同作用的结果；其中一些因素是在殖民统治时期形成的，其他因素则在独立后得到扩展。在独立时，我们继承了那种掠夺性架构，即国家被设计用来控制领土和征收收入，而不是服务于公民。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "DAMILOLA BAKARE argues against the overwhelming vestiges of colonialism Sixty-six years of owning a flag, a constitution, and a seat at the UN. Yet as a single, amalgamated British territory, Nigeria existed for only 46 years (1914–1960). How does 46 years of colonial setup continue to outlive 66 years of self-rule? Nigeria inherited a 46-year-old colonial system, but we have also had six and a half decades to modify it, replace it, or build something entirely different. That is the real paradox. The flag came down, but the wiring remained. The ideas, incentives and hierarchies did not leave with the British. A mindset does not leave because those who installed it left, it must be deliberately replaced, and no one did that for us in 1960. Colonialism was not just about the District Officers and administrative boundaries, it was also about creating a hierarchy of knowledge, authority, language, culture and values. It taught people, over generations, what should be considered sophisticated and local; what was legitimate and what was informal; what was polished and what was not; what counted as knowledge and what was superstition. Those ideas did not require British officials to remain in Nigeria to survive, they were reproduced by Nigerians themselves. That is what makes the idea of mental colonialism worth examining as Nigeria marks 66 years of independence. We see it every day in the courtroom, where lawyers still wear 17th-century British horsehair wigs and black gowns in 35-degree heat to appear learned. You see it in the civil service, where a three-piece suit in 38-degree Abuja remains the uniform of seriousness, while Kaftan or Isiagu is “casual.” Local entrepreneurs struggle to gain legitimacy in their own market, while imported brands are automatically associated with quality and prestige. The message is consistent: authority must look foreign to be real. To be fair, none of this is explained by colonialism alone, globalisation, class, economic incentives and modern media all play their part. But colonial history helped build the hierarchies through which we still assign value. Skin bleaching is widespread across Africa, with Nigeria recording some of the highest reported rates. The exact figures vary, but the pattern is undeniable. Why does lighter skin command such a premium in a society whose overwhelming majority is Black? The answer is not colonialism alone. Colourism, class, gender expectations, advertising, and modern beauty culture all play a role, but colonialism built the framework for these to grow. We see the same pattern in the world of work. Skilled trades are often viewed as backup plan by graduates rather than professions requiring expertise, certification, and entrepreneurship. Yes, higher pay and growth opportunities sometimes explain these preferences, but do they explain why certain forms of work are considered inherently more prestigious even when they pay less than skilled trades? The problem is not aspiration. It is the hierarchy of aspiration. Colonial influence did not survive through one channel, several factors; some created during colonial rule and others expanded after independence account for this phenomenon. Colonial education was closely connected to the administrative and economic needs of the colonial system. It was built to produce clerks not creators. Overtime, the Nigerian elite learned to equate intelligence with fluency in English, not competence in solving Nigerian problems. We inherited that meanwhile; the objective of education should be to produce Nigerians capable of understanding the world and solving Nigerian problems. Indirect rule preserved chiefs but reduced their legitimacy. They became accountable upward to the Crown, not downward to the people. At independence, we inherited that extractive architecture where a state designed to control territory and collect revenue, not to serve citizens. And that is one of the deepest questions of post-colonial Nigeria.",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ng"
   ],
