   "title": "PUBLIC PROCUREMENT AND INDUSTRIAL DEVELOPMENT",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/20/public-procurement-and-industrial-development/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 02:07:57 +0000",
   "_dt": 1792462077.0,
   "ageDays": 0.01,
   "summary": "By pledging to make a strong presentation to the National Economic Council (NEC) on the need for federal and state ministries, departments and agencies (MDAs) to patronise D-PAN vehicles, Sani placed public procurement at the centre of the debate on industrial development. Government procurement can be an important instrument of industrial policy. Public procurement should consider price, quality, safety, durability, after-sales support, maintenance costs and value for money.",
   "summary_zh": "通过承诺向国家经济委员会（NEC）就联邦和州部委、部门和机构（MDAs）需要惠顾D-PAN车辆的问题做出有力陈述，萨尼将公共采购置于工业发展辩论的中心。政府采购可以成为工业政策的重要工具。公共采购应考虑价格、质量、安全、耐用性、售后支持、维护成本和性价比。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Uba Sani pushes for government patronage of Made-in-Nigeria Vehicles, writes KABIR DANJUMA When Governor Uba Sani unveiled the 2026 Peugeot 5008 at the Dangote Peugeot Automobile Nigeria (D-PAN) Limited factory in Kaduna, the event represented more than the introduction of a new vehicle model. It offered an opportunity to renew an important conversation about Nigeria’s industrial future: how government patronage can help sustain local manufacturing, create jobs and turn domestic production into a driver of economic growth. The governor’s message was direct. Nigerian governments should do more than celebrate locally manufactured products; they should create practical demand for them. By pledging to make a strong presentation to the National Economic Council (NEC) on the need for federal and state ministries, departments and agencies (MDAs) to patronise D-PAN vehicles, Sani placed public procurement at the centre of the debate on industrial development. He also pledged that Kaduna State would order some of the vehicles, declaring that his administration intended to lead by example. The proposal raises a fundamental question for Nigeria’s manufacturing sector: what happens when the government becomes an active customer of the industries it hopes to grow? The Peugeot 5008 unveiling provided a platform for the governor to underscore the economic importance of the automobile assembly plant to Kaduna State. Sani commended Alhaji Aliko Dangote for what he described as patriotism through sustained investment in Nigeria. He noted that D-PAN had created jobs, helped address skills gaps and stimulated economic activity within its immediate environment and across the state. These contributions illustrate why manufacturing matters beyond the factory floor. An operating industrial plant creates demand for workers, technical expertise, logistics, maintenance, supplies and other supporting services. Its economic influence can extend into surrounding communities, where businesses and households benefit from the spending generated by industrial activity. For Kaduna, strengthening such an enterprise offers an opportunity to deepen the state’s industrial base while creating pathways for residents to participate in productive economic activity. But industrial investment also requires a dependable market. A factory may possess the equipment, workforce and capacity to assemble vehicles, yet its long-term sustainability depends on customers willing and able to purchase its products. This is where Sani’s call for government patronage becomes particularly significant. Government procurement can be an important instrument of industrial policy. Federal and state institutions require vehicles for official duties, field operations, inspections and administrative responsibilities. Where locally manufactured products meet the necessary requirements, directing a portion of that demand towards domestic producers can support industrial activity. Sani’s proposed engagement with the NEC seeks to extend this argument beyond Kaduna State. His objective is to encourage governments at both federal and state levels to consider D-PAN vehicles when making procurement decisions. His pledge that Kaduna State would place an order is equally important because it gives practical expression to the message he wants other governments to embrace. However, sustainable patronage must be built on more than declarations. Public procurement should consider price, quality, safety, durability, after-sales support, maintenance costs and value for money. Transparent purchasing processes and clear standards would help ensure that patronage supports local industry without compromising public accountability. For D-PAN and other Nigerian manufacturers, the opportunity is to demonstrate that locally produced vehicles can meet the operational requirements of institutional buyers while offering dependable service throughout their working life.",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ng"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "eco",
    "pol"
   ],
   "tier": 4,
   "actor": "第三方",
   "alsoUS": false,
