   "title": "Uduaghan: Life, political battles, controversies",
   "url": "https://dailytrust.com/uduaghan-life-political-battles-controversies/",
   "domain": "dailytrust.com",
   "source": "Daily Trust（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:15:41 +0000",
   "_dt": 1792458941.0,
   "ageDays": 0.05,
   "summary": "He governed the state from 2007 to 2015, a period marked by political controversies, infrastructure projects, sporting achievements and a fierce struggle over his successor. Uduaghan’s eight years in office were marked by political battles, including controversies over his predecessor’s corruption allegations and a struggle to determine who would succeed him. One of the biggest setbacks of Uduaghan’s political career was his failure to install his preferred successor in 2015.",
   "summary_zh": "他从2007年至2015年执政该州，这段时期充满了政治争议、基础设施项目、体育成就以及围绕其继任者的激烈斗争。乌杜阿汉执政八年期间充满了政治斗争，包括其前任腐败指控引发的争议以及确定继任者的斗争。乌杜阿汉政治生涯的最大挫折之一是他在2015年未能让其首选继任者上台。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Former Delta State Governor Emmanuel Uduaghan has died at the age of 71, three days before his 72nd birthday. Uduaghan died on Monday morning, October 19, 2026, according to a statement issued on behalf of the family by Toju Anire Uduaghan. The family described his death as a profound loss, saying he was a source of strength and a father figure whose life touched many people in Delta State and beyond. “His passing has left us devastated. Words can hardly express the depth of our sorrow at the loss of a man who meant so much to our family and whose life and service touched countless others across Delta State and Nigeria,” the statement said. Born on October 22, 1954, Uduaghan was a medical doctor of Itsekiri origin from Warri North Local Government Area of Delta State. He governed the state from 2007 to 2015, a period marked by political controversies, infrastructure projects, sporting achievements and a fierce struggle over his successor. Before becoming governor, he served as Commissioner for Health from May 1999 to May 2003 under former Governor James Ibori. He was later appointed Secretary to the State Government in June 2003, a position he held until the end of Ibori’s administration in 2007. Uduaghan attended Federal Government College, Warri, from 1968 to 1974 and studied medicine at the University of Benin from 1975 to 1980, earning an MBBS degree. He also obtained a diploma in Anaesthesia. Uduaghan’s eight years in office were marked by political battles, including controversies over his predecessor’s corruption allegations and a struggle to determine who would succeed him. His first tenure was briefly interrupted when the Court of Appeal nullified his election shortly before the 2011 general elections. The Speaker of the Delta State House of Assembly, Sam Obi, served as acting governor for about three months before Uduaghan won the rerun election and returned to office. His administration also faced criticism over its handling of allegations against Ibori, who was eventually convicted of corruption-related offences in the United Kingdom and sentenced to prison. Critics accused Uduaghan of failing to investigate and prosecute his predecessor despite allegations and petitions against him. The controversy remained a significant part of the political narrative surrounding his administration. Although some associates described Uduaghan as generous during his years as commissioner and secretary to the state government, others reportedly felt his approach to spending changed after he became governor. Unlike Ibori, whose administration embarked on several ambitious projects, Uduaghan was not widely associated with a similarly expansive construction programme. One explanation offered at the time was that his government faced financial obligations inherited from his predecessor. However, his administration recorded notable infrastructure and sporting achievements. Among its prominent projects were flyovers at Effurun in Warri and along Summit Road in Asaba. The Summit Road flyover was later named after him. His government also sustained Delta State’s strong position in Nigerian sports. The state won the National Sports Festival in successive editions during his administration. Uduaghan’s tenure was also associated with the rise of Delta-born football administrator Amaju Pinnick, who became president of the Nigeria Football Federation. One of the biggest setbacks of Uduaghan’s political career was his failure to install his preferred successor in 2015. He initially backed Tony Obuh, a permanent secretary from Ika, to succeed him. He later dropped that position and supported David Edevbie, an Urhobo politician widely regarded as Ibori’s preferred candidate. The succession battle exposed divisions within the Peoples Democratic Party (PDP), which was then the ruling party in the state. Amid the contest, Ifeanyi Okowa emerged as the party’s governorship candidate and won the general election, succeeding Uduaghan in May 2015.",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
