   "platform": "x",
   "title": "His government is sleepwalking into a serious economic crisis.",
   "url": "https://x.com/thomasjeff007/status/2111869166034579888",
   "snippet": "His government is sleepwalking into a serious economic crisis. The fundamental mistake was abandoning the GH¢16-to-the-dollar exchange rate he inherited and pursuing a currency policy that appears increasingly costly to sustain. He is now reportedly committing roughly $1.2 billion a month in gold-related foreign exchange interventions to keep the cedi around GH¢11 to the dollar, while the economy struggles to create jobs for an estimated 500,000 people entering the labour market every year.\n\nWhat is the point of defending the cedi at such a high cost if the economy is not generating enough productive jobs, expanding exports, and building sustainable sources of foreign exchange? A stronger currency means little if it rests on costly interventions rather than stronger fundamentals.\n\nThis is precisely the kind of economic strategy that demands urgent scrutiny. The government must explain the true cost of these interventions, their sustainability, and the measurable benefits they deliver to ordinary Ghanaians. What is even more troubling is the apparent absence of an urgent push from those closest to the president for a fundamental policy reassessment. Ghana cannot afford to confuse short-term currency stability with genuine economic recovery. The country needs a credible course correction before the costs become even harder to reverse.",
