   "platform": "x",
   "title": "You'd agree with me that the Nigeria’s security strategy looks completely different depending on which part of the country you look at, and",
   "url": "https://x.com/SonofChuks/status/2111766250770543080",
   "snippet": "You'd agree with me that the Nigeria’s security strategy looks completely different depending on which part of the country you look at, and this unfairness is tearing national unity apart. \n\nIn the South West and South South, the federal government works with local security groups and cuts deals with regional leaders to keep the peace and protect oil. \n\nIn the North, the government uses soft approaches like rehabilitation programs to bring former bandits and insurgents back into society and recruiting them into the same Army—a move that many citizens strongly question. \n\nBut in the South East, the strategy completely flips. Instead of cutting deals or supporting local security setups, the government relies almost entirely on heavy military force. When you combine this aggressive stance with the fact that the South East is constantly left out of key government positions, it is easy to see cordinated marginalisation. \n\nIf the military wants everyone to trust and respect it, the government must stop treating different regions with different rules. True security can only happen when there is equal justice for all.",
   "body": "You'd agree with me that the Nigeria’s security strategy looks completely different depending on which part of the country you look at, and this unfairness is tearing national unity apart. \n\nIn the South West and South South, the federal government works with local security groups and cuts deals with regional leaders to keep the peace and protect oil. \n\nIn the North, the government uses soft approaches like rehabilitation programs to bring former bandits and insurgents back into society and recruiting them into the same Army—a move that many citizens strongly question. \n\nBut in the South East, the strategy completely flips. Instead of cutting deals or supporting local security setups, the government relies almost entirely on heavy military force. When you combine this aggressive stance with the fact that the South East is constantly left out of key government positions, it is easy to see cordinated marginalisation. \n\nIf the military wants everyone to trust and respect it, the government must stop treating different regions with different rules. True security can only happen when there is equal justice for all.",
